<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</h1><p class="st-meta">1482 CE – 1769 CE · capital Bhaktapur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three Malla kingdoms of the Kathmandu Valley, whose palace square and five-storey temple are masterpieces of Newar architecture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaksha-malla">Yaksha Malla</a> of the Malla dynasty died in 1482, his kingdom was divided among his sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raya-malla">Raya Malla</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, the old capital in the east of the valley, while his brothers established the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a> and other seats, creating rival Malla kingdoms in a small area.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> was the most traditional of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar</a> kingdoms, rich in festivals and crafts such as pottery and woodcarving. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupatindra-malla">Bhupatindra Malla</a>, around 1700, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatapola-temple">Nyatapola</a> temple and enlarged the fifty-five-window palace, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranajit-malla">Ranajit Malla</a> added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-gate-bhaktapur">Golden Gate</a> in 1754. The kingdom controlled routes to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> in the east and competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a> for trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> in 1769, after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranajit-malla">Ranajit Malla</a>, its last king, went into exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. Bhaktapur's medieval town survives as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-bhaktapur.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Bhaktapur" width="320" height="206"><figcaption>Image: Nyeta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Bhaktapur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1482 CE – 1769 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bhaktapur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Raya Malla, Bhupatindra Malla, Ranajit Malla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Newar Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Newari (Nepal Bhasa)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nepal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bhadgaon, Bhaktapur Malla kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
